implementationPlan.description:
ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the final step. Load adversarial-self-review. Read the diff as a launch that wants to run something other than the protected runtime.

1. Mutants. Apply each in a scratch copy of the step-5 snapshot, never to the shared files. Run the trimmed sh harness, and the ps1 one where it is reachable. Each mutant must turn the named row red:
   (a) no gate: P1;
   (b) an existence test only, with no absolute check: P3;
   (c) `-e`, or Test-Path without Leaf, accepting a directory: P4;
   (d) the gate checks only MOE_PROXY_PATH: P2;
   (e) any non-empty marker protects: P8;
   (f) the gate moved after ensure_mcp_config or after the MCP temp config: P2 (a config is written before the daemon refusal);
   (g) the message prints the value: P1;
   (h) a protected success that prefers `command -v moe-proxy` or Get-MoeInstallPath: P6.
   Afterwards restore, cmp, and rerun green.
2. Resolution paths: in both wrappers, every proxy or daemon resolution either takes the exact override or refuses under protection. That includes TEAM_PROXY, moe_rpc, the codex/gemini/grok TOML writers and the heartbeat sidecar, which are all fed from PROXY_ARGS / $proxyScript.
3. Legacy is byte-identical. Diff the legacy branches of both wrappers against HEAD: the legacy-visible change must be none.
4. Edge cases:
   - a value with spaces or quotes (ps1 Trim) stays a single argument;
   - a symlink to a regular file passes [ -f ] / Test-Path Leaf, which is accepted, because the launcher already validates real files;
   - Git Bash drive paths;
   - a hot reload re-runs the gate.
5. Concurrency: the gate is per process and reads only its own env and the filesystem, so seats cannot interfere with each other. A file deleted after the gate is refused at its use site, not fallen back from.

Record the findings and fixes in the step note.
